.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt RT_SIGQUEUEINFO 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rt_sigqueueinfo ,
.Nm rt_tgsigqueueinfo
.Nd send a signal with its siginfo
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Ft int
.Fn rt_sigqueueinfo "pid_t pid" "int sig" "siginfo_t *info"
.Ft int
.Fn rt_tgsigqueueinfo "pid_t tgid" "pid_t tid" "int sig" "siginfo_t *info"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn rt_sigqueueinfo ,
number 178 on i386 and 129 on x86-64, which the C libraries'
.Fn sigqueue
calls, sends
.Fa sig
to the process
.Fa pid
with the code of
.Fa info
and the three words after it, the sender's pid and uid and the value,
which a handler and
.Xr sigtimedwait 2
are given.
.Fn rt_tgsigqueueinfo ,
number 335 on i386 and 297 on x86-64, sends it to the thread
.Fa tid
of the process
.Fa tgid
alone, as
.Xr tgkill 2
does.
The permission is that of
.Xr kill 2 .
A code of zero or above, or
.Dv SI_TKILL ,
is the kernel's, and a caller may give it only to its own process, as on
Linux.
A signal of 0 checks the target and sends nothing.
.Pp
The kernel keeps one pending instance of each signal, so a real-time
signal sent again while it is pending is merged with it, where Linux
queues each.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
0, or -1 with
.Va errno
set.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EINVAL
.It Er EFAULT
.Fa info
is not in the caller's memory.
.It Er EINVAL
.Fa sig
is outside 0 to 64, or for
.Fn rt_tgsigqueueinfo
an id is not positive.
.It Er EPERM
The caller may not signal the target, or gave a kernel's code to another
process.
.It Er ESRCH
No process or thread has the id, or the thread is not one of
.Fa tgid Ns 's .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kill 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2 ,
.Xr tgkill 2
